# Design — web-phase-grid-spans (In progress phase grid, spanning parent cards)

Compact design: the three questions the research left open, the geometry, and
the view-model/markup/CSS shape. Follows the research at
`tasks/web-phase-grid-spans/research`.

## The three open questions, decided

1. **What a spanning card counts as.** A lane's `Count` is *the in-progress
   tasks whose own phase that lane is* — not "the tasks on its cards". A root
   card contributes itself to its own phase lane; each nested in-progress
   subtask contributes itself to *its* own phase lane, even when it is drawn
   inside a parent card that sits in another lane. A nested todo subtask still
   counts in To do only.
   Invariant kept and tested: every in-progress task has exactly one own
   phase, so the four lane counts partition the column count.
   Rationale: it is the question a phase header asks ("how many tasks are in
   research right now"), and it stays a partition once a card can straddle
   lanes, which "tasks on its cards" no longer can.

2. **Lane grouping.** A card is listed under the lane it *begins* in
   (`LaneFrom`), so the vertical stack is ordered by where the eye enters the
   card. That is its own phase for a card without in-progress subtasks, and
   the earliest phase of its group otherwise. Cards and counts therefore
   describe different things on purpose; both are documented on
   `PhaseLaneView`.

3. **Header shape.** The four lane headers stay as they are: a header row per
   lane, placed at its own lane's single-lane width, with the count chip at
   its right edge. They become grid items of the one grid instead of boxes of
   four stacked sections; an empty lane stays a header-only stub. The
   `11rem` / `14rem` thresholds keep their meaning (below), so no new ones.

## Geometry: four overlapping lanes on seven tracks

Lane *k* starts at *k·s* (`s` = one lane step); a single-lane card is
`C = 100% - 3s` wide, so a card ending in lane *m* has its right edge at
`m·s + C`. The eight distinct grid lines are therefore

    0, s, 2s, 3s, 100%-3s, 100%-2s, 100%-s, 100%

i.e. seven tracks: `s s s (100%-6s) s s s`, the middle one `minmax(0, 1fr)`
absorbing the rest. A card spanning lanes *k..m* is

    grid-column: (k+1) / (m+5)

which is one class on each side — `.lane-from-<phase>` sets
`grid-column-start` (1..4), `.lane-to-<phase>` sets `grid-column-end` (5..8).
Go names phases; it computes no pixels and no track numbers, exactly as
`--lane` came from `.lane-<phase>` before.

`--lane-step: clamp(0px, (100% - var(--lane-min)) / 3, 16.666%)`:

- The `16.666%` cap is forced by the middle track (`100% - 6s >= 0`), and it
  makes a single-lane card half the column with a step of a third of a card
  (before: card 40%, step 20% — a half card). A four-lane span is then exactly
  the column width, which is the point of the grid.
- `--lane-min: 11rem` keeps its meaning: the step shrinks so a single-lane
  card never goes below it, reaching 0 at an 11rem column.
- `@container lanes (width < 14rem)` still drops the whole thing to one track
  and a plain stack, now by overriding `grid-template-columns` and resetting
  `grid-column` on the two placed kinds.

Rejected again (as in `tasks/in-progress-phase-columns/design:527`): five
equal 20% tracks. It reproduces today's card/step ratio but has no way to
shrink the step independently of the card, so it has no minimum width — the
seven-track form is what buys that back.

## Subtask rows

Each nested subtask row is indented by `(its lane - the card's first lane)`
steps, as `.sub-lane` + `.sub-lane-<0..3>` (`--sub-lane: N`, the `--lane`
idiom). The step is written in container units —
`clamp(0px, (100cqi - var(--lane-min)) / 3, 16.666cqi)` — so it resolves
against `.lanes`, which is the inline-size container, and not against the
card's own width; the pitch of the staircase inside a card therefore matches
the grid's. It is a pitch match, not pixel alignment: the card's padding
offsets every row by the same amount.

A nested row with no phase of its own (a todo subtask) gets offset 0. Outside
`.lanes` (detail view, other columns) `LaneOffset` is never set, so the
margin is `0 * <length>` = 0 and `cqi` never matters.

## View model

- `CardView.LaneFrom` / `LaneTo`: phase names, the span. Set for In progress
  root cards only; equal when the card has no in-progress subtasks.
- `CardView.LaneOffset`: int 0..3, a nested row's indent in lane steps from
  the parent card's first lane.
- `PhaseLaneView` unchanged in shape; `Count` and `Cards` get the new
  documented meanings from questions 1 and 2.
- `newPhaseLanes` computes the span, fills the offsets and buckets by
  `LaneFrom`. It annotates the cards in place, so the In progress branch of
  `newBoardView` calls it before `deref`.

## Markup

`.lanes` is the grid. Each lane stays a `<section class="lane"
data-phase="<phase>">` with `display: contents`, so its header and its cards
become grid items of `.lanes` while the section survives as the grouping and
test hook. Sparse auto-placement puts every item on its own row in document
order (each item's column start is left of the cursor), so the column stays
one vertical stack. The per-lane `flex flex-col gap-2` card stack is gone —
`row-gap` on the grid replaces it — and `lane-<phase>` leaves the section's
class list, since it no longer carries geometry.

## Tests

- `TestLaneGroupTakesFurthestPhase` is replaced by a spanning test: the card
  is in its earliest lane, `LaneFrom`/`LaneTo` cover the group, and the two
  lane counts are 1 each.
- `TestLanesPartitionTheColumn` keeps the sum invariant under the new count
  rule, and gains a spanning card.
- New: subtask `LaneOffset`, including a todo subtask at 0.
- Markup: the section's new class list, a spanning card's
  `lane-from-*`/`lane-to-*` pair, a shifted subtask row.
- `assets_test.go` swaps the `.lane-<phase>` assertions for
  `.lane-from-<phase>` / `.lane-to-<phase>` and the `.sub-lane` rules.
